  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1128 PM CDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\n05Z synopsis shows spotty showers and storms located from the \nColorado border to near Liberal as a 500-700 mb shortwave is moving \ninto western Kansas due to northwest flow aloft.  \n\nShort term models have the upper level wave, cloud cover, and any \nrain exiting the region by mid morning.  Winds throughout the day \nshould be light with a looser pressure gradient across western \nKansas.  By late afternoon hotter air from the southwest should \narrive into far western Kansas and with ample sunshine NBM \nprobabilities of hitting 100 degrees are at 40% for areas along and \nsouth of highway 160.  With continued northwest flow in the mid \nlevels another 500-700 mb shortwave will develop in the front range \nin the late afternoon triggering convection.  With the upper level \nhigh breaking down to the southwest this should allow the convection \nto reach into western Kansas after sunset and the storms will decay \nas they move eastward through the night.\n\nTuesday the day should start off with residual cloud cover in the \nmorning as the shortwave moves onto the east and more easterly \nsurface winds.  This will keep temperatures at bay through the \nmorning but by afternoon with the return of sunshine and southerly \nwinds we should see the return of mid 90s.  By mid to late afternoon \nshort term models have a stout mid level shortwave coming from \nsouthern Nebraska into north central Kansas.  We could see storm \ndevelopment as early as 4 pm in our eastern zones.  Another front \nrange shortwave should trigger a line of convection in eastern \nColorado and move into western Kansas during the overnight hours. \nPOP has increased with the newest model guidance to 50-80%. NBM \nprobabilities continue to show a 40-50% chance of at least 0.25 inch \nof precipitation west of highway 283 suggesting this round of storms \nwill have better lift and more moisture to work with. MUCAPE values \nfrom various models Wednesday morning are in the 2000-3000 range and \nskew t soundings suggest elevated convection with not much bulk \nshear which would lead to mainly a wind threat and localized heavier \nrainfall with the stronger storms.\n\nThe persistent weather pattern continues into Wednesday night as \nmedium range models show another late night shortwave passing \nthrough Kansas giving much of western Kansas another decent shot of \nrain with POPs in the 40-50% range.  The repeated opportunities for \nrain along with the upper high departing slightly westward should \nallow highs to be slightly cooler mid to late week with highs in the \nlower 90s.  Long term ensembles have the upper high moving back to \nthe east during the weekend allowing a higher probability of hotter \nweather to return.  NBM probabilities of 100 degree highs are back \ninto the 40-90% range for much of western Kansas for Saturday and \nSunday.",
